package cajeta.net.http;

/**
 * **Server-side request limits + hardening knobs** — plan item
 * **NET-9.6**. Where [`HttpParserLimits`](HttpParserLimits.cajeta) (NET-7.3)
 * bounds the *head* the [`HttpParser`](HttpParser.cajeta) buffers, this type
 * bounds the rest of a request's resource envelope the
 * [`HttpServer`](HttpServer.cajeta) (NET-9.1 / NET-9.4) reads: how long a
 * peer may take to deliver the head, how long the body may take, how large
 * the body may grow, and whether the server honours `Expect: 100-continue`.
 *
 * These four ceilings close the abuse vectors the plan's threat table names
 * for the server (`plan/cajeta-net-plan.md` §Threats — "HTTP parser abuse
 * (header floods, slowloris, smuggling)"): the head/body **deadlines** evict
 * a slowloris client that dribbles bytes to pin a worker forever, the body
 * **size cap** rejects an over-large upload with `413` before it is
 * buffered, and the `100-continue` toggle lets the server gate a large body
 * on an interim `100 Continue` it can decline with `417` first.
 *
 * ## The four ceilings
 *
 *   - **`headReadTimeoutMs`** — the wall-clock budget (milliseconds) for a
 *     peer to deliver a **complete request head** (request-line + all
 *     header lines + the terminating blank line) once the first byte of a
 *     new request arrives. The **slowloris** guard: a client that sends one
 *     byte every few seconds, never completing the head, is timed out
 *     ([`TimedOutException`](../TimedOutException.cajeta) →
 *     [`HttpServer`](HttpServer.cajeta) closes the connection) instead of
 *     holding the worker indefinitely. A non-positive value disables the
 *     head deadline (unbounded — the v1 NET-9.1 behaviour).
 *   - **`bodyReadTimeoutMs`** — the wall-clock budget (milliseconds) for the
 *     **whole body** to arrive after the head completes. The body-side twin
 *     of the head deadline — a slow-body trickle is bounded too. A
 *     non-positive value disables the body deadline.
 *   - **`maxBodyBytes`** — the largest message body the server will accept,
 *     in bytes. A declared `Content-Length` over this ceiling is rejected up
 *     front with [`PayloadTooLargeException`](PayloadTooLargeException.cajeta)
 *     (`413 Payload Too Large`) **before** any body byte is buffered; a
 *     `chunked` body that *streams* past the ceiling is rejected the moment
 *     the decoded total crosses it. A non-positive value disables the body
 *     size cap.
 *   - **`expectContinueEnabled`** — when `true` (the default), a request
 *     carrying `Expect: 100-continue` is answered by the
 *     [`ExpectContinue`](ExpectContinue.cajeta) policy: an interim
 *     `100 Continue` (HTTP/1.1) telling the client to send the body, or a
 *     `417 Expectation Failed` when the request would be rejected anyway
 *     (e.g. its `Content-Length` already exceeds `maxBodyBytes`) so the
 *     client never sends the body. When `false`, the server ignores the
 *     `Expect` header (the client must decide on its own timeout).
 *
 * ## Defaults
 *
 * The defaults mirror a conservative public-facing server posture: a 30 s
 * head deadline (generous for a human-paced client, fatal to a slowloris),
 * a 60 s body deadline, a 16 MiB body cap (the common reverse-proxy default,
 * e.g. nginx `client_max_body_size`), and `100-continue` honoured. A caller
 * tunes them via the [`of`](#of) factory; a `0` for any numeric field
 * selects "unbounded / disabled" rather than that field's default, so a
 * caller opts *out* of a ceiling explicitly.
 *
 * This is a **pure value type** — no I/O — so it tests directly over the
 * JIT alongside the parser limits, and the
 * [`HttpServer`](HttpServer.cajeta) pure byte path
 * ([`handleRequestWithLimits`](HttpServer.cajeta#handleRequestWithLimits))
 * enforces the body-size + expect ceilings without a live socket.
 */
public class ServerLimits {
    /** Default head-read deadline: 30 s. The slowloris guard. */
    public static final int32 DEFAULT_HEAD_TIMEOUT_MS = 30000;
    /** Default body-read deadline: 60 s. */
    public static final int32 DEFAULT_BODY_TIMEOUT_MS = 60000;
    /** Default max body size: 16 MiB. */
    public static final int64 DEFAULT_MAX_BODY_BYTES = (int64) 16777216;

    /**
     * Milliseconds a peer may take to deliver a complete request head; a
     * non-positive value disables the head deadline. The slowloris guard.
     */
    public int32 headReadTimeoutMs;
    /**
     * Milliseconds the whole body may take after the head completes; a
     * non-positive value disables the body deadline.
     */
    public int32 bodyReadTimeoutMs;
    /**
     * Largest accepted body in bytes; a non-positive value disables the
     * size cap. A `Content-Length` over this → `413` up front; a `chunked`
     * stream over this → `413` mid-stream.
     */
    public int64 maxBodyBytes;
    /**
     * Honour `Expect: 100-continue` (default `true`) — see
     * [`ExpectContinue`](ExpectContinue.cajeta).
     */
    public boolean expectContinueEnabled;

    /**
     * Field-pinning constructor at the conservative public-server defaults:
     * a 30 s head deadline, a 60 s body deadline, a 16 MiB body cap, and
     * `100-continue` honoured.
     */
    public ServerLimits() {
        this.headReadTimeoutMs = ServerLimits.DEFAULT_HEAD_TIMEOUT_MS;
        this.bodyReadTimeoutMs = ServerLimits.DEFAULT_BODY_TIMEOUT_MS;
        this.maxBodyBytes = ServerLimits.DEFAULT_MAX_BODY_BYTES;
        this.expectContinueEnabled = true;
    }

    /**
     * Explicit-ceiling factory. A non-positive value for any numeric field
     * is taken **verbatim** as "disabled / unbounded" (unlike
     * [`HttpParserLimits.of`](HttpParserLimits.cajeta#of), which normalizes
     * a non-positive to its default) — a server hardening policy must be
     * able to *remove* a ceiling deliberately, so a caller passes `0` to
     * opt out rather than restate a default.
     */
    public static #ServerLimits of(int32 headReadTimeoutMs,
                                   int32 bodyReadTimeoutMs,
                                   int64 maxBodyBytes,
                                   boolean expectContinueEnabled) {
        ServerLimits l = heap ServerLimits();
        l.headReadTimeoutMs = headReadTimeoutMs;
        l.bodyReadTimeoutMs = bodyReadTimeoutMs;
        l.maxBodyBytes = maxBodyBytes;
        l.expectContinueEnabled = expectContinueEnabled;
        return #l;
    }

    /** True iff a head-read deadline is in force (positive budget). */
    public boolean hasHeadDeadline() {
        return this.headReadTimeoutMs > 0;
    }

    /** True iff a body-read deadline is in force (positive budget). */
    public boolean hasBodyDeadline() {
        return this.bodyReadTimeoutMs > 0;
    }

    /** True iff a body-size ceiling is in force (positive cap). */
    public boolean hasBodyCap() {
        return this.maxBodyBytes > 0;
    }

    /**
     * Whether a body of `lengthBytes` is within the configured cap. Always
     * `true` when [`hasBodyCap`](#hasBodyCap) is `false` (no ceiling). The
     * comparison the [`HttpServer`](HttpServer.cajeta) body path applies
     * both to a declared `Content-Length` (up front) and to a running
     * `chunked` total (mid-stream).
     */
    public boolean bodyWithinCap(int64 lengthBytes) {
        if (!this.hasBodyCap()) {
            return true;
        }
        return lengthBytes <= this.maxBodyBytes;
    }
}
